WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:09.880
U.S. education has been marked by fierce debates
over whether schools should group students

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.320
based on ability.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:15.940
Critics have suggested that U.S. schools engage
in “ability grouping” to an unhealthy,

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.270
undemocratic degree.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:21.369
So, does the U.S. really do a lot more of
this than do other nations?

00:00:21.369 --> 00:00:24.779
Well, a recent OECD analysis is clarifying.

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:30.239
In 2006, across 40 nations, just nine
percent of 15-year-olds were enrolled

00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:32.390
in schools that had ability grouping.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:35.340
In 2016, that figure was eight percent.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:36.340
Remarkable.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:37.340
Just eight percent.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.140
And the U.S.?

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:43.440
The comparable figure was four percent, in
both 2006 and 2016.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.539
Yep, the U.S. engaged in ability grouping
at just half the international rate.

00:00:48.539 --> 00:00:49.819
It’s funny.

00:00:49.819 --> 00:00:54.449
Our education debates tend to be extremely
bitter, with critics of American schools

00:00:54.449 --> 00:00:58.469
insisting that our policies should be more like those elsewhere.

00:00:58.469 --> 00:01:04.170
Yet, at least in this case, it turns
out that U.S. schools are wholly innocent

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:05.400
of the charge at hand.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:09.540
Do you think it's a problem if schools group students by ability?

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:10.940
Let us know in our poll.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:15.620
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:20.420
and be sure to like and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

